        ":question/id": "54404730-020a-547b-8cd2-8751476ed4d0",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-13613",
        ":question/problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-23x+10?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $10$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }10}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $3$ does not take the above form. The value $3$ can be interpreted as a fraction $\\frac{3}{1},$ and the numerator $(3)$ is not a factor of $10.$"
      }
